-- Employment Claims Act 2016 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition: PART 3, EMPLOYMENT CLAIMS, Divisions 1 and 2 (ss 8 to 17).
-- Layer 1: the law, objectively.  Target compiler: main (build 109).
--
-- Reading a result: JUST TRUE is a yes the Act gives; JUST FALSE is a no the Act gives in its own words; NOTHING is "not covered" (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").
-- Everything the Act leaves to regulations, Rules of Court or practice directions (the classes of employee and employer, the period in s 12(6), the claim limits in s 12(7), forms, fees) is a MAYBE input: NOTHING when it is not given.
-- ss 8 to 11 (establishment, appointments, the Registry) are encoded as small decision functions below; s 12(8) is a regulation-making power: machinery (inert, reason 2), no rule here.

§ `Part 3 -- Employment claims, Divisions 1 and 2`

§§ `ss 8 to 11 -- the tribunals, their magistrates and the Registry`

-- s 8: the President may, under section 4 of the State Courts Act 1970, constitute one or more Employment Claims Tribunals.
-- src:811 | 8. The President may, under section 4 of the State Courts Act 1970,
-- src:812 | constitute one or more Employment Claims Tribunals.
@ref s 8 (src:811-812)
GIVEN `the person is the President` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8 the person may constitute one or more Employment Claims Tribunals under section 4 of the State Courts Act 1970` MEANS
    IF `the person is the President`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [scope limit, ruled (c)]: the power is given to "The President"; another person is not spoken to
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 9(1): a tribunal is presided over by a tribunal magistrate appointed by the President on the Chief Justice's recommendation, or designated under subsection (5).
-- src:816 | 9.—(1) A tribunal is presided over by —
-- src:818 | (a) a tribunal magistrate appointed by the President on the
-- src:819 | recommendation of the Chief Justice; or
-- src:821 | (b) a tribunal magistrate designated under subsection (5).
@ref s 9(1) (src:816-821)
GIVEN `the individual was appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Chief Justice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the individual was designated under subsection (5)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(1) the individual presides over a tribunal as a tribunal magistrate` MEANS
    IF `the individual was appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Chief Justice` OR `the individual was designated under subsection (5)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "A tribunal is presided over by" (a) or (b) and does not say that no one else may preside
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 9(2): an individual is eligible for appointment under (1)(a) if the individual is a qualified person as defined in section 2(1) of the Legal Profession Act 1966.
-- src:823 | (2) An individual is eligible for appointment under
-- src:824 | subsection (1)(a) as a tribunal magistrate if the individual is a
-- src:825 | qualified person as defined in section 2(1) of the Legal Profession
-- src:826 | Act 1966.
@ref s 9(2) (src:823-826)
GIVEN `the individual is a qualified person as defined in section 2(1) of the Legal Profession Act 1966` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(2) the individual is eligible for appointment as a tribunal magistrate` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the individual is a qualified person as defined in section 2(1) of the Legal Profession Act 1966`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the meaning of "qualified person" is in section 2(1) of the Legal Profession Act 1966, which is not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST qualified THEN
        IF qualified
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An individual is eligible for appointment under subsection (1)(a) as a tribunal magistrate if the individual is a qualified person" (the individual is not one)
        ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 9(3): an individual appointed under (1)(a) holds office for the term in the instrument of appointment, and may be re-appointed.
-- src:828 | (3) An individual appointed under subsection (1)(a) as a tribunal
-- src:829 | magistrate holds office for a term specified in the individual’s
-- src:830 | instrument of appointment, and may be re‑appointed.
@ref s 9(3) (src:828-830)
GIVEN `the individual was appointed under subsection (1)(a) as a tribunal magistrate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(3) the individual may be re-appointed as a tribunal magistrate` MEANS
    IF `the individual was appointed under subsection (1)(a) as a tribunal magistrate`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [scope limit, ruled (c)]: the sentence is about "An individual appointed under subsection (1)(a)"; another individual is not spoken to (the term itself is whatever the instrument of appointment says, and is not an input of any function here)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 9(4): despite (3), the President may, on the Chief Justice's recommendation, at any time revoke an appointment under (1)(a).
-- src:835 | (4) Despite subsection (3), the President may, on the
-- src:836 | recommendation of the Chief Justice, at any time revoke the
-- src:837 | appointment under subsection (1)(a) of a tribunal magistrate.
@ref s 9(4) (src:835-837)
GIVEN `the appointment was made under subsection (1)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Chief Justice has recommended the revocation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(4) the President may revoke the appointment of the tribunal magistrate` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [scope limit, ruled (c)]: the power is over "the appointment under subsection (1)(a)"; a designation under subsection (5) is not spoken to
        IF (NOT `the appointment was made under subsection (1)(a)`) THEN NOTHING
        IF `the Chief Justice has recommended the revocation` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the President may, on the recommendation of the Chief Justice, at any time revoke" [assumed: when] (there is no recommendation)
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 9(5): the Presiding Judge of the State Courts may designate any District Judge as a tribunal magistrate.
-- src:839 | (5) The Presiding Judge of the State Courts may designate any
