§§ `ss 9 and 11 -- who exercises a tribunal's jurisdiction`

-- s 9(6): a person who was appointed or designated as a tribunal magistrate may still sit to give judgment on a case that person heard.
-- src:842 | (6) Any individual appointed under subsection (1)(a) or designated
-- src:843 | under subsection (5) as a tribunal magistrate may, although the period
-- src:844 | of the individual’s appointment or designation has expired, or the
-- src:845 | individual’s appointment or designation has been revoked, sit as a
-- src:846 | tribunal magistrate for the purpose of giving judgment or otherwise in
-- src:847 | relation to any case heard by the individual as a tribunal magistrate.
@ref s 9(6) (src:842-847)
GIVEN `the individual was appointed under section 9(1)(a) or designated under section 9(5) as a tribunal magistrate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the individual heard the case as a tribunal magistrate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(6) the individual may sit as a tribunal magistrate in relation to the case` MEANS
    IF `the individual was appointed under section 9(1)(a) or designated under section 9(5) as a tribunal magistrate` AND `the individual heard the case as a tribunal magistrate`
    -- (the appointment or designation having expired or been revoked does not matter: "although the period ... has expired, or ... has been revoked")
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [scope limit, ruled (c)]: the permission is for "any case heard by the individual as a tribunal magistrate"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 11(3): the jurisdiction and powers of a tribunal may be exercised by the registrar for the tribunals, to the extent authorised by this Act or by Rules of Court made under section 33.
-- src:863 | (3) To the extent authorised by this Act or by Rules of Court made
-- src:864 | under section 33, the jurisdiction and powers of a tribunal may be
-- src:865 | exercised by the registrar for the tribunals.
@ref s 11(3) (src:863-865)
GIVEN `the exercise is authorised by this Act or by Rules of Court made under section 33` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(3) the registrar for the tribunals may exercise the jurisdiction and powers of a tribunal` MEANS
    IF `the exercise is authorised by this Act or by Rules of Court made under section 33`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [scope limit, ruled (c)]: "To the extent authorised by this Act or by Rules of Court" limits the extent of the grant and carries no "only"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 11(4): all the powers and duties of the registrar for the tribunals may be exercised and performed by a deputy registrar or an assistant registrar for the tribunals.
-- src:867 | (4) All the powers and duties of the registrar for the tribunals may
-- src:868 | be exercised and performed by a deputy registrar or an assistant
-- src:869 | registrar for the tribunals.
@ref s 11(4) (src:867-869)
GIVEN office IS AN `Office for the tribunals`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(4) the officer may exercise and perform the powers and duties of the registrar for the tribunals` MEANS
    CONSIDER office
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the permission names a deputy registrar and an assistant registrar; it says nothing of the registrar's own exercise of the registrar's powers
    WHEN `The registrar` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `A deputy registrar` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `An assistant registrar` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [scope limit, ruled (c)]: the permission names a deputy registrar and an assistant registrar for the tribunals; the clause names who may act and carries no "only"
    WHEN `Another officer` THEN NOTHING

§§ `s 12 -- jurisdiction of a tribunal`

-- s 12(1): it does not matter when the dispute arose.
-- src:875 | 12.—(1) Subject to this Act, a tribunal has jurisdiction to hear and
-- src:876 | determine any claim in relation to which all applicable requirements
-- src:881 | set out in subsections (2) to (7) are satisfied, regardless whether the
-- src:882 | dispute giving rise to the claim arose before, on or after 1 April 2017.
@ref s 12(1) (src:875-882)
GIVEN `the date on which the dispute giving rise to the claim arose` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(1) the date on which the dispute arose does not affect the tribunal's jurisdiction` MEANS
    -- "regardless whether the dispute giving rise to the claim arose before, on or after 1 April 2017": every date gives the same answer, so the date is read and not used
    JUST TRUE

-- helper: the matters s 12(2)(c)(i) names.  A classification by the Schedules' own numbering, not a legal answer.
-- src:905 | (i) any matter specified in item 17 of the First Schedule
-- src:906 | or item 14 or 16 of the Second Schedule; or
@ref s 12(2)(c)(i) (src:905-906)
GIVEN matter IS A `Matter of a dispute`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(2)(c)(i) the matter is specified in item 17 of the First Schedule or item 14 or 16 of the Second Schedule` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `A matter in the First Schedule` item THEN `sch 1 item number of` item EQUALS "17"
    WHEN `A matter in the Second Schedule` item THEN `sch 2 item number of` item EQUALS "14" OR `sch 2 item number of` item EQUALS "16"
    WHEN `A matter in the Third Schedule` item THEN FALSE
    WHEN `None of the matters in the Schedules` THEN FALSE

-- s 12(2)(a): the claim is made by an employee, or the person entitled in the employee's place, against the employer; the employee is prescribed or in a prescribed class.
-- src:886 | (a) by an employee who is prescribed or belongs to a
-- src:887 | prescribed class of employees (or a person entitled under
-- src:888 | any written law to receive an amount claimed (if any) in
-- src:889 | place of the employee) against the employer of the
-- src:890 | employee;
@ref s 12(2)(a) (src:886-890)
GIVEN who IS A `Who makes the claim`
      against IS A `Whom the claim is made against`
      `the employee is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(2)(a) the claim is made by an employee in a prescribed class against the employer` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The claim must be made —" by an employee (or the person entitled in the employee's place) against the employer of the employee; this claim is not made so
        IF NOT ((who EQUALS `The employee` OR who EQUALS `A person entitled under a written law to receive the amount claimed in place of the employee`) AND against EQUALS `The employer of the employee`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): who is "prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees" is in regulations that are not among the sources
        IF `the employee is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        IF `the employee is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "by an employee who is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 12(2)(b): the claim is made against a person liable under section 65 of the Employment Act 1968 for the employee's salary.
-- src:892 | (b) by an employee who is prescribed or belongs to a
-- src:893 | prescribed class of employees (or a person entitled under
-- src:894 | any written law to receive the amount claimed in place of
-- src:895 | the employee) against a person liable under section 65 of
-- src:896 | the Employment Act 1968 for the employee’s salary; or
@ref s 12(2)(b) (src:892-896)
GIVEN who IS A `Who makes the claim`
      against IS A `Whom the claim is made against`
      `the employee is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(2)(b) the claim is made by an employee in a prescribed class against a person liable under section 65 of the Employment Act 1968` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The claim must be made —" by an employee (or the person entitled in the employee's place) against a person liable under section 65 of the Employment Act 1968; this claim is not made so
        IF NOT ((who EQUALS `The employee` OR who EQUALS `A person entitled under a written law to receive the amount claimed in place of the employee`) AND against EQUALS `A person liable under section 65 of the Employment Act 1968 for the employee's salary`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): who is "prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees" is in regulations that are not among the sources
        IF `the employee is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        IF `the employee is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "by an employee who is prescribed or belongs to a prescribed class of employees"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- helper: whether a matter is one the First Schedule or the Second Schedule specifies.  A classification by the Schedules' own numbering, not a legal answer.
GIVEN matter IS A `Matter of a dispute`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(2)(c) the matter is specified in the First or Second Schedule` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `A matter in the First Schedule` item THEN TRUE
    WHEN `A matter in the Second Schedule` item THEN TRUE
    WHEN `A matter in the Third Schedule` item THEN FALSE
    WHEN `None of the matters in the Schedules` THEN FALSE

-- s 12(2)(c): the claim is made by an employer, or the person entitled in the employer's place, against an employee, for an amount relating to a specified employment dispute about a matter in (i) or (ii).
-- src:898 | (c) by an employer who is prescribed or belongs to a
