    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 17 -- transfer of proceedings`

-- s 17(5): "appropriate court" means the General Division of the High Court, a District Court or a Magistrate's Court.
-- src:1247 | (5) In this section, “appropriate court” means the General Division
-- src:1248 | of the High Court, a District Court or a Magistrate’s Court.
@ref s 17(5) (src:1247-1248)
GIVEN court IS A `Court named in the Act`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 17(5) the court is an appropriate court` MEANS
    CONSIDER court
    WHEN `The General Division of the High Court` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `A District Court` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `A Magistrate's Court` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate court” means the General Division of the High Court, a District Court or a Magistrate’s Court"
    WHEN `Another court` THEN JUST FALSE

-- s 17(1): on a party's application, an appropriate court may order the proceedings or a counterclaim transferred to it if it appears there is sufficient reason.
-- src:1220 | 17.—(1) Where it appears to an appropriate court, on the
-- src:1221 | application of a party to any proceedings before a tribunal, that
-- src:1222 | there is sufficient reason for those proceedings, or a counterclaim in
-- src:1223 | those proceedings, to be dealt with by that court, that court may order
-- src:1228 | those proceedings or that counterclaim (as the case may be) to be
-- src:1229 | transferred to that court.
@ref s 17(1) (src:1220-1229)
GIVEN `the court is an appropriate court` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `a party to the proceedings before the tribunal applies to the court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it appears to the court that there is sufficient reason for the proceedings, or a counterclaim in them, to be dealt with by that court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 17(1) the court may order the proceedings or the counterclaim to be transferred to it` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [scope limit, ruled (c)]: the power is given to "an appropriate court" (section 17(5)); another court is not spoken to
        IF `the court is an appropriate court` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the court is an appropriate court was not given
        IF NOT (`the court is an appropriate court` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING
        IF `a party to the proceedings before the tribunal applies to the court` AND `it appears to the court that there is sufficient reason for the proceedings, or a counterclaim in them, to be dealt with by that court` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where it appears to an appropriate court, on the application of a party to any proceedings before a tribunal, that there is sufficient reason" [ruled (b)] (no party has applied, or there is no sufficient reason)
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 17(2): the application must be made within the time and in the manner prescribed by Rules of Court.
-- src:1231 | (2) An application under subsection (1) must be made within the
-- src:1232 | time and manner prescribed by Rules of Court made under section 33.
@ref s 17(2) (src:1231-1232)
GIVEN `the application is made within the time and in the manner prescribed by Rules of Court made under section 33` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 17(2) the application is made as required` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the application is made within the time and in the manner prescribed by Rules of Court made under section 33`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the time and manner are in Rules of Court that are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST asRequired THEN
        IF asRequired
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An application under subsection (1) must be made within the time and manner prescribed by Rules of Court"
        ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 17(3): an order under (1) may be made on any terms the court thinks fit.
-- src:1234 | (3) An order under subsection (1) may be made on any terms that
-- src:1235 | the appropriate court thinks fit.
@ref s 17(3) (src:1234-1235)
`s 17(3) the court may make the order on any terms it thinks fit` MEANS JUST TRUE

-- s 17(4): where the court orders a counterclaim transferred and the tribunal determines the claim by ordering the respondent to pay money, execution on the order is stayed until the counterclaim proceedings are concluded, unless that court otherwise orders.
-- src:1237 | (4) Where the appropriate court orders under subsection (1) that a
-- src:1238 | counterclaim in proceedings before a tribunal be transferred to that
-- src:1239 | court, and the tribunal determines the claim in those proceedings by
-- src:1240 | ordering the respondent to pay money to the claimant, unless that
-- src:1241 | court at any time otherwise orders, execution or enforcement on the
-- src:1242 | tribunal’s order is stayed until the proceedings on the counterclaim
-- src:1243 | are concluded.
@ref s 17(4) (src:1237-1243)
GIVEN `the appropriate court orders a counterclaim in proceedings before a tribunal to be transferred to that court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the tribunal determines the claim by ordering the respondent to pay money to the claimant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that court at any time otherwise orders` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings on the counterclaim are concluded` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 17(4) execution or enforcement of the tribunal's order is stayed` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the appropriate court orders ... that a counterclaim ... be transferred ..., and the tribunal determines the claim ... by ordering the respondent to pay money" [ruled (b)] (one of the two has not happened)
        IF NOT (`the appropriate court orders a counterclaim in proceedings before a tribunal to be transferred to that court` AND `the tribunal determines the claim by ordering the respondent to pay money to the claimant`) THEN JUST FALSE
        IF (NOT `that court at any time otherwise orders`) AND (NOT `the proceedings on the counterclaim are concluded`) THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "execution or enforcement on the tribunal's order is stayed until the proceedings on the counterclaim are concluded", "unless that court at any time otherwise orders" (the court has otherwise ordered, or the proceedings are concluded)
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
